(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["governance",{"c:eu-ai-act-risk-tiers-and-obligations":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>EU AI Act\u003c/b> (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) regulates AI by risk. A few practices are \u003cb>banned\u003c/b>, some uses are \u003cb>high-risk\u003c/b> and carry heavy obligations, some systems have \u003cb>transparency\u003c/b> duties, and most AI is \u003cb>minimal risk\u003c/b> with no new rules. General-purpose models have their own track. It applies to anyone placing AI on the EU market or whose AI output is used in the EU, wherever they are based. The dates below are as of late 2026, after the 2026 \"AI Omnibus\" amendments.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Who you are under the Act\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Obligations depend on your \u003cb>role\u003c/b>. A \u003cb>provider\u003c/b> develops an AI system (or has one developed) and places it on the market or puts it into service under its own name. A \u003cb>deployer\u003c/b> uses an AI system under its authority in a professional context. Importers and distributors have smaller duties. If you build an LLM app and sell it, you are its provider; the company using it internally is the deployer. A deployer becomes a provider of a high-risk system if it puts its own name on it, substantially modifies it, or repurposes it for a high-risk use.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The four tiers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tier\u003c/th>\u003cth>What is in it\u003c/th>\u003cth>What you must do\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prohibited (Article 5)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Social scoring; manipulative or deceptive techniques that cause significant harm; exploiting vulnerabilities due to age, disability or economic situation; predicting crime from profiling alone; untargeted scraping of face images for recognition databases; emotion recognition at work or in education (except medical or safety reasons); biometric categorisation to infer sensitive traits; real-time remote biometric identification in public for policing (narrow exceptions); from 2 December 2026, systems generating non-consensual intimate imagery or child sexual abuse material\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Do not build or use it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>High-risk\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Safety components of products under EU product law (Annex I: medical devices, machinery, toys, lifts) and the Annex III uses: biometrics; critical infrastructure; education (admissions, grading, proctoring); employment (CV screening, promotion, termination, task allocation, monitoring); access to essential services (public benefits, credit scoring, life and health insurance pricing, emergency call triage); law enforcement; migration and border control; justice and democratic processes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full set of provider and deployer obligations (below)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Transparency (Article 50)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Systems that talk to people; systems generating synthetic audio, images, video or text; emotion recognition and biometric categorisation; deepfakes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tell people they are talking to AI; mark generated content in a machine-readable way; label deepfakes and AI-written text published to inform the public (unless a human has editorial responsibility)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Minimal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Everything else: spam filters, coding assistants, most internal tools and chatbots that are not in the lists above\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No new obligations beyond AI literacy; voluntary codes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An Annex III system can escape the high-risk tier if it poses no significant risk, for example because it performs a narrow procedural task, improves the result of work a human already completed, or only prepares an assessment a human makes. The provider must document that reasoning, and any Annex III system that \u003cb>profiles\u003c/b> people is always high-risk.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>High-risk obligations in one view\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Providers\u003c/b> need a risk management system, data governance for training and test data, technical documentation, automatic logging, instructions for deployers, designed-in human oversight, appropriate accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity, a quality management system, a conformity assessment and CE marking, registration in the EU database, and post-market monitoring with serious-incident reporting.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Deployers\u003c/b> must use the system according to its instructions, assign competent people to oversee it, monitor it, keep its logs for at least six months, inform workers and people affected by its decisions, and in some cases (public bodies, and deployers of credit scoring or life and health insurance pricing) carry out a fundamental rights impact assessment. People affected by certain high-risk decisions have a right to an explanation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The timeline as of late 2026\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Date\u003c/th>\u003cth>What applies\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1 Aug 2024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The Act enters into force\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Feb 2025\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prohibitions and AI literacy\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Aug 2025\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Obligations for general-purpose AI models; governance bodies; national penalty rules\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Aug 2026\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most remaining rules, including Article 50 transparency; enforcement starts for GPAI models, prohibitions, transparency and AI literacy\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Dec 2026\u003c/td>\u003ctd>New ban on generating non-consensual intimate imagery and CSAM; deadline for generative systems already on the market before 2 Aug 2026 to meet the content-marking rule\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Aug 2027\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GPAI models placed on the market before 2 Aug 2025 must comply\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Dec 2027\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High-risk rules for Annex III uses\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Aug 2028\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High-risk rules for AI in Annex I products\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-note\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Note\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The AI Omnibus.\u003c/b> The Commission proposed targeted amendments on 19 November 2025 as part of its Digital Omnibus simplification package. Parliament and Council reached political agreement on 7 May 2026 and the amending regulation entered into force on 27 July 2026. It moved the high-risk deadlines from 2 August 2026 (Annex III) and 2 August 2027 (Annex I) to the dates above, added the ban on nudification and CSAM generators, softened the AI literacy duty (providers and deployers must take measures to support it, without having to guarantee a level), extended SME simplifications to small mid-cap companies, explicitly allowed processing sensitive personal data to detect and correct bias, and strengthened the AI Office's oversight of systems built on general-purpose models. Expect more guidance and standards; check the Service Desk before relying on any date.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Penalties\u003c/b> reach up to EUR 35 million or 7% of worldwide annual turnover for prohibited practices, EUR 15 million or 3% for most other breaches, and EUR 7.5 million or 1% for supplying incorrect information; for SMEs the lower amount applies.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How engineers use this\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Classify every AI feature at design time and record the reasoning: role, tier, any Annex III exception, transparency duties. The Commission's online Compliance Checker on the Service Desk is a good first pass. Most LLM features land in the minimal or transparency tiers; the expensive surprises come from features that quietly drift into Annex III, such as a \"helpful\" summary of candidates that starts ranking them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Classify five use cases into the Act's risk tiers: a shop's customer-support chatbot, a CV-ranking tool for recruiters, webcam emotion detection for call-centre staff, an internal coding assistant, and a credit-scoring model for consumer loans.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The support chatbot is a transparency-tier system: users must be told they are talking to AI (applies since 2 August 2026). CV ranking is high-risk under Annex III employment, with full provider and deployer obligations from 2 December 2027. Emotion recognition of workers is prohibited (since 2 February 2025) unless it is for medical or safety reasons. The internal coding assistant is minimal risk (AI literacy only). Consumer credit scoring is high-risk under Annex III essential services from 2 December 2027, and the deployer must also run a fundamental rights impact assessment.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You build an LLM app that a bank uses internally. Who is the provider and who is the deployer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You are the provider, because you develop it and place it on the market under your name; the bank is the deployer. If the bank rebrands it or repurposes it for a high-risk use such as credit decisions, the bank can become the provider of that high-risk system.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What did the 2026 AI Omnibus change about high-risk deadlines?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It moved the start of high-risk obligations for Annex III uses from 2 August 2026 to 2 December 2027, and for AI in Annex I regulated products from 2 August 2027 to 2 August 2028. It entered into force on 27 July 2026.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:general-purpose-ai-model-obligations":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>general-purpose AI (GPAI) model\u003c/b> is a model that can competently perform a wide range of tasks and be built into many systems, such as the LLMs behind every API. Since 2 August 2025 their \u003cb>providers\u003c/b> must document the model, share information with downstream builders, respect EU copyright law and publish a training-data summary. The few models with \u003cb>systemic risk\u003c/b> must also be evaluated, red-teamed and monitored. Most AI engineers are \u003cb>downstream providers\u003c/b> who build on these models, and only become GPAI providers themselves if they modify a model with very large amounts of compute.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What counts as a GPAI model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The Act defines GPAI models by generality. The Commission's July 2025 guidelines add a practical indicator: a model trained with more than 10^23 floating-point operations (FLOP) that can generate language (text or audio), text-to-image or text-to-video. A large model specialised for one job (transcription, weather forecasting) is excluded if it lacks broad capabilities.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What every GPAI provider must do (Article 53)\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Technical documentation\u003c/b> for the AI Office and national authorities: architecture, training process, data, compute, energy use, evaluation results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Information for downstream providers\u003c/b> so they understand capabilities and limits and can meet their own obligations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A copyright policy\u003c/b>, including respecting machine-readable opt-outs from text and data mining (for example in \u003ccode>robots.txt\u003c/code> or metadata).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A public summary of training content\u003c/b>, using the Commission's template: main datasets, top domain names scraped, and how data was processed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Models released under a free and open-source licence with public weights are exempt from the two documentation duties, unless they have systemic risk. The copyright policy and training summary still apply.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Systemic risk (Articles 51 and 55)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A model is presumed to have \u003cb>systemic risk\u003c/b> when its training used more than 10^25 FLOP, or the Commission designates it. Its provider must notify the Commission, run state-of-the-art evaluations including adversarial testing, assess and mitigate systemic risks (for example to public health, security or critical infrastructure), track and report serious incidents, and ensure strong cybersecurity for the model and its weights.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The Code of Practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Published on 10 July 2025, the voluntary \u003cb>GPAI Code of Practice\u003c/b> gives providers a recognised way to show compliance. It has three chapters: \u003cb>Transparency\u003c/b> (with a Model Documentation Form), \u003cb>Copyright\u003c/b>, and \u003cb>Safety and Security\u003c/b> (only for systemic-risk models). The Commission and the AI Board confirmed it as adequate. Signatories include Amazon, Anthropic, Google, IBM, Microsoft, Mistral AI and OpenAI among others; xAI signed only the Safety and Security chapter, and Meta is not on the list. Non-signatories must show compliance by other means.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Dates and enforcement\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Date\u003c/th>\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Aug 2025\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Obligations apply to GPAI models placed on the market from this date\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Aug 2026\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The AI Office can enforce: request documentation, evaluate models, order fixes and fine providers up to 3% of worldwide turnover or EUR 15 million\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2 Aug 2027\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Deadline for models already on the market before 2 Aug 2025\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where you fit as an engineer\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Using a model through an API to build a product\u003c/b> makes you a downstream provider of an AI system. You have no GPAI duties; you rely on the model provider's documentation, and your obligations come from your system's own risk tier (transparency, high-risk or minimal).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Fine-tuning or otherwise modifying a model\u003c/b> makes you the provider of the modified GPAI model only if the modification uses more than a third of the compute used to train the original (the guidelines' indicative threshold). Your obligations then cover only the modification: its documentation, its training data summary and copyright compliance.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Training compute ≈ 6 × parameters × training tokens\n\nOriginal: 7B model trained on 2T tokens\n  6 × 7e9 × 2e12 = 8.4e22 FLOP\nLoRA-style fine-tune on 100M tokens through the same 7B model\n  at most 6 × 7e9 × 1e8 = 4.2e18 FLOP\n\n4.2e18 / 8.4e22 = 0.00005, far below one third\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Typical fine-tuning is tens of thousands of times below the threshold. Continued pretraining of a large model on hundreds of billions of tokens is where you need to do the arithmetic properly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep the model provider's documentation (model card, usage policies, training summary) in your system file; you will need it for your own technical documentation and system card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Record every base model and its version in your inventory; a model swap changes your documentation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If you publish open-weight models, read the training-summary template and the copyright chapter before release, not after.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Summarise what providers and deployers of GPAI must do.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every GPAI model provider must keep technical documentation for the AI Office, give downstream providers the information they need, have a policy to comply with EU copyright law including text-and-data-mining opt-outs, and publish a training-content summary using the Commission's template (open-source models are exempt from the two documentation duties unless they have systemic risk). Providers of systemic-risk models (presumed above 10^25 FLOP) must also notify the Commission, evaluate and red-team the model, mitigate systemic risks, report serious incidents and secure the model. Companies that build or deploy systems on top of a GPAI model have no GPAI duties of their own; they follow the rules for their system's risk tier (for example Article 50 transparency or high-risk obligations) plus AI literacy, using the model provider's documentation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A startup LoRA fine-tunes an open 8B model on 50 million tokens. Does it become a GPAI provider?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No. Its fine-tuning compute (about 6 × 8e9 × 5e7 ≈ 2.4e18 FLOP) is a tiny fraction of the original training compute, far below the one-third indicator, so it remains a downstream provider and its obligations depend on how its system is used.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the role of the Code of Practice?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is a voluntary, Commission-endorsed way for GPAI providers to demonstrate compliance with their transparency, copyright and (for systemic-risk models) safety and security obligations, giving signatories more legal certainty than proving compliance by other means.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:nist-ai-rmf-govern-map-measure-manage":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>NIST AI Risk Management Framework\u003c/b> (AI RMF) is a voluntary US framework for managing AI risk across an organisation and a system's life cycle. It has four functions: \u003cb>Govern\u003c/b> (who is accountable and how), \u003cb>Map\u003c/b> (understand the context and the risks), \u003cb>Measure\u003c/b> (test and track them) and \u003cb>Manage\u003c/b> (treat, monitor and respond). It is the most common structure for AI risk registers, inside and outside the US.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Status as of late 2026\u003c/h4>\u003cp>AI RMF 1.0 was released on 26 January 2023, with a companion \u003cb>Playbook\u003c/b> of suggested actions. In July 2024 NIST added the \u003cb>Generative AI Profile\u003c/b> (NIST AI 600-1). NIST states that AI RMF 1.0 is being revised as part of the White House AI Action Plan, and in April 2026 it published a concept note for a profile on AI in critical infrastructure. The four functions are the stable core.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What makes AI trustworthy\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The framework lists seven characteristics your controls should support: \u003cb>valid and reliable\u003c/b>; \u003cb>safe\u003c/b>; \u003cb>secure and resilient\u003c/b>; \u003cb>accountable and transparent\u003c/b>; \u003cb>explainable and interpretable\u003c/b>; \u003cb>privacy-enhanced\u003c/b>; and \u003cb>fair, with harmful bias managed\u003c/b>. They trade off against each other (more explanation can reduce privacy; more safety filtering can reduce usefulness), and the framework asks you to make those trade-offs deliberately.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The four functions\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Govern\u003c/b>\u003csmall>policies, roles, accountability\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Map\u003c/b>\u003csmall>context, impacts, risks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Measure\u003c/b>\u003csmall>tests, metrics, monitoring\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Manage\u003c/b>\u003csmall>treat, respond, improve\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Function\u003c/th>\u003cth>Key questions\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical artefacts\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Govern\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Who owns AI risk? What policies apply? How are concerns raised?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>AI policy, roles and approvals, model inventory, training, incident process\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What is the system for, who uses it, who could be harmed, what could go wrong?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Use-case description, stakeholder and impact analysis, risk register, legal classification\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Measure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How do we know how bad each risk is, now and over time?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Eval suites, red-team reports, bias tests, monitoring dashboards\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Manage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What do we do about each risk, and what happens when it materialises?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mitigation plan, go or no-go decisions, incident response, decommissioning plan\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Govern is cross-cutting: it sets up the people and processes that make the other three happen. Map, Measure and Manage repeat for every system and every significant change.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The generative AI risks\u003c/h4>\u003cp>NIST AI 600-1 lists twelve risks that generative AI creates or makes worse: chemical, biological, radiological or nuclear (CBRN) information; \u003cb>confabulation\u003c/b> (confidently stated false content); dangerous, violent or hateful content; data privacy; environmental impacts; harmful bias and homogenisation; human-AI configuration (over-reliance, automation bias); information integrity; information security; intellectual property; obscene, degrading or abusive content; and value chain and component integration (opaque third-party models and data). It is a useful checklist for the Map step.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: a RAG assistant over internal documents\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Function\u003c/th>\u003cth>What you do for this system\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Govern\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Name an owner; record the system in the model inventory; agree an acceptable-use policy; define who approves launches and model swaps; set an incident process\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Users are employees; data includes HR and customer documents; risks: confabulated answers, leaking documents across permission boundaries, prompt injection via edited pages, PII in logs, uneven quality for non-English documents, dependency on one provider\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Measure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Groundedness and correctness evals on 200 questions; permission-leak tests; injection red-team suite; PII scan of traces; per-language quality; weekly sample review of production traces\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Manage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieval filtered by user permissions; citations required; guardrails and redaction; fallback provider; launch gate on eval thresholds; alerting on groundedness drops; rollback plan; quarterly review\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Teams usually implement the RMF as a \u003cb>risk register\u003c/b>: one row per risk with owner, likelihood, impact, controls, metrics and status, reviewed on a schedule.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The functions map cleanly onto the engineering you already do: Measure is your evals and red-teaming; Manage is your launch gates, monitoring and incident response.\u003c/li>\u003cli>NIST publishes crosswalks to other frameworks such as ISO/IEC 42001, so one register can serve several audits.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Map your RAG build's risks to the four functions.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Govern: an owner, an inventory entry, an acceptable-use policy and a launch and model-change approval process. Map: identify users and affected people, the data involved and the risks, such as confabulated answers, cross-permission document leaks, prompt injection through edited pages, PII in logs and weaker quality in some languages. Measure: groundedness and correctness evals, permission-leak and injection tests, PII scans of traces, per-language metrics and weekly trace review. Manage: permission-filtered retrieval, required citations, guardrails and redaction, launch gates on eval thresholds, alerts, a fallback provider, rollback and incident response.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is Govern described as cross-cutting?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It creates the culture, policies, roles and accountability that make mapping, measuring and managing happen at all, so it applies across every system and every stage rather than being one step in the sequence.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name four of the twelve generative AI risks in NIST AI 600-1.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any four of: CBRN information; confabulation; dangerous, violent or hateful content; data privacy; environmental impacts; harmful bias and homogenisation; human-AI configuration; information integrity; information security; intellectual property; obscene, degrading or abusive content; value chain and component integration.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:iso-iec-42001-ai-management-systems":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>ISO/IEC 42001\u003c/b> (published December 2023) is the first international standard for an \u003cb>AI management system\u003c/b> (AIMS): the policies, roles, processes and records an organisation uses to develop, provide or use AI responsibly. Like ISO 27001 for information security, it is certifiable by accredited auditors. It certifies the \u003ci>organisation's way of working\u003c/i>, not any single model or product.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A management system, not a product test\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A management system is a loop: set policies and objectives, run processes to meet them, check the results, improve. ISO calls this \u003cb>Plan-Do-Check-Act\u003c/b>. ISO/IEC 42001 uses the same high-level structure as other ISO management system standards, so a company already certified for ISO 27001 can extend its existing processes (document control, internal audit, management review) rather than start again.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What you must have: the clauses\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Clause\u003c/th>\u003cth>Requirement in plain words\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4 Context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Define the scope of the AIMS, your role (developer, provider, user of AI), interested parties and their requirements, including laws such as the EU AI Act\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>5 Leadership\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Top management commitment, an AI policy, clear roles and responsibilities\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>6 Planning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An AI risk assessment and risk treatment process, AI system impact assessments, measurable AI objectives, and a \u003cb>Statement of Applicability\u003c/b> saying which Annex A controls you apply and why\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>7 Support\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Resources, competent people, awareness, communication and controlled documentation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>8 Operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Actually run the risk assessments, treatments and impact assessments for your AI systems, and control changes and suppliers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>9 Performance evaluation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monitoring and measurement, internal audits and management reviews\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10 Improvement\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Handle nonconformities with corrective action and improve continually\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Annex A: the reference controls\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Annex A lists controls you consider for your Statement of Applicability, grouped under these objectives:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Policies related to AI\u003c/b> and their review.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Internal organisation:\u003c/b> roles, responsibilities and a way to report concerns.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Resources for AI systems:\u003c/b> data, tools, compute and people documented.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Assessing impacts\u003c/b> of AI systems on individuals, groups and society.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>AI system life cycle:\u003c/b> requirements, design, verification and validation, deployment, operation and monitoring, technical documentation and event logs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data for AI systems:\u003c/b> quality, provenance, acquisition and preparation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Information for interested parties:\u003c/b> user documentation, incident communication.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use of AI systems:\u003c/b> responsible and intended use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Third-party and customer relationships:\u003c/b> allocating responsibilities with suppliers and customers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Annex B gives implementation guidance for each control. Related standards fill in detail: ISO/IEC 23894 (AI risk management guidance), ISO/IEC 42005 (AI system impact assessment, 2025) and ISO/IEC 22989 (terminology).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How certification works\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Gap analysis\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Build the AIMS\u003c/b>\u003csmall>policy, risk process, controls\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Run it\u003c/b>\u003csmall>records for months\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Internal audit and management review\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Stage 1 audit\u003c/b>\u003csmall>documents\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Stage 2 audit\u003c/b>\u003csmall>evidence it works\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Certificate\u003c/b>\u003csmall>annual surveillance audits\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An accredited certification body runs a \u003cb>stage 1 audit\u003c/b> (is the documented system complete and ready?) and a \u003cb>stage 2 audit\u003c/b> (is it working, with evidence?). Certificates typically last three years with yearly surveillance audits. Since July 2025, \u003cb>ISO/IEC 42006\u003c/b> sets the extra requirements for the bodies that audit and certify against 42001, so auditors must show AI-specific competence.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where engineers come in\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Auditors ask for evidence, and most of it comes from engineering:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>a \u003cb>model and system inventory\u003c/b> with owners and versions;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>risk and impact assessments\u003c/b> per system (the NIST-style register from the previous lesson works);\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>eval and red-team reports\u003c/b> with dates and thresholds;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>change records\u003c/b> for prompts, models and data;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>data provenance\u003c/b> records for training, fine-tuning and retrieval corpora;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>monitoring dashboards and incident tickets\u003c/b>, showing that issues were found and fixed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>If your evals, traces and system cards already exist (earlier modules), certification is mostly about organising them and proving the loop runs. If they do not, no amount of policy writing will pass stage 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Treating certification as proof of legal compliance. ISO/IEC 42001 helps you show good governance, but it does not by itself demonstrate conformity with the EU AI Act's product requirements.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Scoping too narrowly (\"only the chatbot team\") so the certificate says little about how the company really builds AI.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Writing policies that nobody follows; stage 2 auditors sample real records.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain what an organisation must have to be certified against ISO/IEC 42001.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A working AI management system with: a defined scope and understanding of its context and role; top management commitment, an AI policy and assigned responsibilities; an AI risk assessment and treatment process, AI system impact assessments, objectives and a Statement of Applicability for the Annex A controls; competent people, resources and controlled documentation; operational processes that actually perform those assessments and manage changes and suppliers; monitoring, internal audits and management reviews; and corrective action with continual improvement. It must then pass an accredited body's stage 1 (documentation) and stage 2 (evidence of operation) audits, followed by annual surveillance audits.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a Statement of Applicability?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A document listing each Annex A control, whether you apply it, how, and why any excluded control does not apply. It links your risk treatment decisions to concrete controls and is a core audit artefact.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Does an ISO/IEC 42001 certificate show that a product complies with the EU AI Act?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No. It shows the organisation runs an AI management system. The AI Act's obligations attach to specific systems and models (risk tier, documentation, conformity assessment), which you must meet separately, although the management system helps produce the evidence.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:model-and-system-cards":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>model card\u003c/b> documents a trained model: what it is for, how it was trained, how it performs and where it fails. A \u003cb>system card\u003c/b> documents a deployed AI \u003ci>system\u003c/i>: the models plus prompts, retrieval, tools, guardrails, oversight and monitoring. As an AI engineer you mostly write system cards, built on the model cards of the models you use. They serve users, auditors, regulators and your own future team.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Model cards\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Margaret Mitchell and colleagues proposed model cards in 2018 (published in 2019) as short documents shipped with every model. Their sections remain the standard skeleton: \u003cb>model details\u003c/b>, \u003cb>intended use\u003c/b> (and out-of-scope uses), \u003cb>factors\u003c/b> (groups and conditions that may affect performance), \u003cb>metrics\u003c/b>, \u003cb>evaluation data\u003c/b>, \u003cb>training data\u003c/b>, \u003cb>quantitative analyses\u003c/b> broken down by those factors, \u003cb>ethical considerations\u003c/b>, and \u003cb>caveats and recommendations\u003c/b>. A companion idea, \u003cb>datasheets for datasets\u003c/b> (Gebru and colleagues, 2018), does the same for data: motivation, composition, collection process, preprocessing, uses, distribution and maintenance.\u003c/p>\u003cp>On Hugging Face the model card is the repository's \u003ccode>README.md\u003c/code>. A YAML header holds machine-readable metadata (licence, languages, datasets, base model, metrics) used for search and licence checks; the Markdown body holds the narrative sections.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>System cards\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Frontier labs publish system cards with major model releases, describing safety evaluations, red-teaming results and mitigations for the model \u003ci>as deployed\u003c/i>. For an application you build, the system card answers the questions a reviewer, customer or regulator will ask. A practical template:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>SYSTEM CARD: Atlas support assistant      version 1.4      2026-10-28\nOwner: support-platform team (atlas-owner@corp.example)\n\n1. Purpose and users      What it does, for whom, in which channels\n2. Out of scope           Uses it must not be relied on for\n3. Legal classification   EU AI Act role and tier, reasoning, other laws\n4. Architecture           Models and versions, providers and regions,\n                          retrieval corpus, tools and their permissions\n5. Data                   Personal data processed, where it flows,\n                          retention, processors and agreements\n6. Evaluation             Datasets, metrics, results, thresholds,\n                          results broken down by language and user group\n7. Known limitations      Failure modes found in error analysis\n8. Safety and security    Guardrails, red-team results, residual risks\n9. Human oversight        Approvals, escalation, override, disclosures\n10. Monitoring            Dashboards, alerts, incident process\n11. Change log            Model, prompt and data changes with dates\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Writing one that is useful\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Be specific and quantitative.\u003c/b> \"Grounded answers on 94% of 200 eval questions (English 96%, German 88%)\" beats \"performs well\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Report breakdowns.\u003c/b> Averages hide the groups for whom the system works badly; the original model card paper insisted on disaggregated results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>State limitations honestly.\u003c/b> Known failure modes, with examples, protect users and you. A card that lists none is not believed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Write for two audiences.\u003c/b> A one-paragraph plain-language summary for users and support staff; detail for auditors and engineers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Version it with the code.\u003c/b> Keep the card in the repository, update it in the same pull request as a model or prompt change, and have CI fail if the model version in config and card disagree.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>How cards connect to regulation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The documentation duties in the EU AI Act overlap heavily with a good system card: high-risk providers need technical documentation and instructions for deployers, and GPAI providers fill in a model documentation form. NIST's Map and Measure functions and ISO/IEC 42001's documentation controls ask for the same facts. Write the card well once and reuse it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Write a system card for one of your builds: what goes in it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For example, for a RAG support assistant: its purpose, users and out-of-scope uses; the EU AI Act role and tier with reasoning; the architecture (models and versions, providers and regions, corpus, tools and their permissions); the personal data processed, flows, retention and processor agreements; evaluation datasets, metrics, thresholds and results broken down by language or user group; known limitations with examples; guardrails, red-team results and residual risks; human oversight, escalation and user disclosures; monitoring, alerts and incident process; and a dated change log, plus a named owner and version.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a model card report results broken down by groups and conditions?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Aggregate metrics can hide poor performance for specific groups, languages or conditions; disaggregated results reveal who the model fails and are needed to judge fairness and fitness for a given use.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you stop a system card going stale?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep it in the repository next to the code, update it in the same pull request as model, prompt or data changes, and add a CI check that the versions it lists match the deployed configuration.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:privacy-and-data-protection":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Privacy law, led by the EU's \u003cb>GDPR\u003c/b>, applies to every LLM feature that touches personal data: prompts, retrieved documents, logs, memory, eval sets and fine-tuning data. The engineering answer is a \u003cb>data-flow design\u003c/b> that collects less, sends less, keeps data for less time and controls who sees it, backed by the right agreements with providers. Privacy is far cheaper to design in than to retrofit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>GDPR in ten minutes, for engineers\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Personal data\u003c/b> is any information about an identifiable person, including pseudonymised data where someone holds the key. \u003cb>Special categories\u003c/b> (health, ethnicity, religion, sexual orientation, biometrics and others) need extra protection.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The \u003cb>controller\u003c/b> decides why and how data is processed (usually your company); a \u003cb>processor\u003c/b> processes it on the controller's behalf (your model provider, vector database host, tracing vendor). Each processor needs a \u003cb>data processing agreement\u003c/b> (Article 28).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every processing purpose needs a \u003cb>lawful basis\u003c/b>: consent, contract, legal obligation, vital interests, public task or legitimate interests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Core principles: \u003cb>purpose limitation\u003c/b>, \u003cb>data minimisation\u003c/b>, accuracy, \u003cb>storage limitation\u003c/b>, security, and accountability (you must be able to show compliance).\u003c/li>\u003cli>People have rights to access, correct and erase their data and to object; \u003cb>Article 22\u003c/b> limits decisions based solely on automated processing that have legal or similarly significant effects.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A \u003cb>data protection impact assessment\u003c/b> (DPIA, Article 35) is required for high-risk processing, which many new AI uses involve.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>International transfers\u003c/b> outside the EU need a legal mechanism such as an adequacy decision or standard contractual clauses.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>What is special about LLM systems\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Every prompt is a transfer to a processor.\u003c/b> Check that the provider does not train on your data (major providers' business APIs do not by default, but confirm the terms), how long it retains requests (often a limited period for abuse monitoring, with zero-data-retention options for eligible customers), and where it processes data.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Models can memorise.\u003c/b> In December 2024 the European Data Protection Board's Opinion 28/2024 said a model trained on personal data is not automatically anonymous: it is anonymous only if extracting personal data from it, including through queries, is very unlikely. It also said legitimate interest can be a lawful basis for developing and deploying models if a three-step test is passed, and that unlawful processing during development can affect the lawfulness of later use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Erasure is hard inside weights.\u003c/b> You cannot simply delete one person from a fine-tuned model. Keep personal data in retrieval indexes and databases, where you can delete it, rather than training it into weights.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Copies multiply.\u003c/b> Traces, caches, memory stores, eval datasets and analytics each hold copies. Each needs a retention period and a deletion path.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>A privacy-preserving data flow\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Minimise\u003c/b>\u003csmall>only needed fields\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Pseudonymise\u003c/b>\u003csmall>names, emails, IDs\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Provider\u003c/b>\u003csmall>DPA, no training, region, retention\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Restore placeholders\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Every hop\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Redacted traces\u003c/b>\u003csmall>30-day retention\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Deletion path\u003c/b>\u003csmall>by user ID\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Element\u003c/th>\u003cth>Control\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Context assembly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fetch only the fields the task needs; never paste whole customer records\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Before the provider\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pseudonymise direct identifiers; keep the mapping in your service\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Provider\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Data processing agreement, no training on your data, region chosen, retention or zero-data-retention agreed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Filter by the user's permissions; index deletion when a source document is deleted\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Store facts per user with timestamps; users can view and delete; expire stale items\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Logs and traces\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Redact at write time; short retention; restricted access\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Evals and fine-tuning data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Built from redacted or synthetic data; documented source and lawful basis\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sensitive cases\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Self-hosted open-weight model when data must not leave your network\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Run a DPIA early for any feature that processes customer or employee data at scale, profiles people or touches special categories; the AI Act's fundamental rights impact assessment, where required, can build on it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep a \u003cb>record of processing\u003c/b> per AI feature: purposes, data categories, processors, retention.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tell users plainly what is processed and by whom in your privacy notice and in the product.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test deletion end to end: delete a test user and check the database, index, memory store, traces and caches.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you design the data flows of an LLM feature to minimise and redact personal data?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start from the task and include only the fields it needs when assembling context. Pseudonymise direct identifiers before the provider call, keep the mapping in your own service and restore values in the answer. Use providers under a data processing agreement with no training on your data, a chosen processing region and limited or zero retention, or a self-hosted model for the most sensitive data. Filter retrieval by user permissions, keep personal data in deletable stores rather than fine-tuned weights, redact traces and logs at write time with short retention, build eval and training sets from redacted or synthetic data, and provide a tested deletion path across database, index, memory, traces and caches.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Under GDPR, what is your model provider usually, and what document do you need with it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A processor acting on your instructions; you need a data processing agreement (Article 28) covering purposes, security, sub-processors, retention and transfers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why prefer retrieval over fine-tuning for personal data?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Data in an index or database can be corrected or deleted on request; data trained into weights cannot be reliably removed and may be memorised and regurgitated.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:licences-for-models-data-and-outputs":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An AI product sits on three layers of rights: the \u003cb>models\u003c/b> you use (licences or API terms), the \u003cb>data\u003c/b> you train, tune or retrieve with (licences and copyright), and the \u003cb>outputs\u003c/b> you generate (who owns them and whether they infringe). Each layer can block a commercial launch. Keep an inventory with the exact licence of every component, checked before you depend on it, and re-checked when versions change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Models\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Licence type\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\u003cth>Watch for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Permissive\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Apache 2.0, MIT\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Attribution and notice files; Apache 2.0 includes a patent grant\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Custom open-weight\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Llama community licences\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Llama 4's licence: a separate licence from Meta if your products had over 700 million monthly active users; an acceptable use policy; \"Built with Llama\" display; derivative model names must start with \"Llama\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Responsible AI licences\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenRAIL family\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Use restrictions that must be passed on to anyone you share the model with\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Non-commercial\u003c/td>\u003ctd>CC BY-NC and research-only licences\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No commercial use at all, including internal business use in many readings\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Closed APIs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider terms of service\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output ownership, data use, bans on using outputs to train competing models, acceptable use policies\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Licences change between versions from the same lab, and a fine-tune inherits its base model's licence. Check the licence on the exact model card and revision you deploy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Data\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Datasets carry their own licences: \u003cb>CC BY\u003c/b> (credit the source), \u003cb>CC BY-SA\u003c/b> (share-alike: derivatives under the same terms), \u003cb>CC BY-NC\u003c/b> (non-commercial), ODC licences for databases, or custom terms such as \"research only\". Web-scraped collections often have unclear rights, because the dataset's licence covers the compilation, not the copyright in each page.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Training on copyrighted works is regulated differently by region:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>EU:\u003c/b> a text-and-data-mining exception allows commercial mining unless the rightholder has opted out in a machine-readable way; the AI Act requires GPAI providers to respect those opt-outs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>US:\u003c/b> it turns on fair use, decided case by case. In June 2025, in \u003ci>Bartz v. Anthropic\u003c/i>, a federal judge found training on lawfully bought and scanned books to be fair use but not the building of a library from pirated copies; the case then settled for 1.5 billion US dollars. Other cases were still open as of late 2026.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>For your own projects the practical rules are: know where every training, fine-tuning, eval and retrieval dataset came from; keep its licence with it; and do not put data you have no right to use into an index that serves customers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Outputs\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ownership.\u003c/b> Major providers' terms generally assign to you whatever rights exist in outputs, but rights may not exist: the US Copyright Office's January 2025 report concluded that purely AI-generated material is not copyrightable, while human contributions (selection, arrangement, substantial edits) can be. Prompts alone are usually not enough.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Infringement.\u003c/b> Outputs can still reproduce protected text, code or images, especially well-known works the model memorised. Generated code may echo code under a copyleft licence such as the GPL.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Indemnities.\u003c/b> Several large providers offer to defend enterprise or API customers against copyright claims over outputs, usually only if you used their safety filters and did not deliberately prompt for infringing content. Read the conditions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Restrictions.\u003c/b> Many API terms forbid using outputs to develop competing models, which matters if you plan to distil a provider's model into your own.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Building the inventory\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from huggingface_hub import HfApi\n\napi = HfApi()\nMODELS = [\"&lt;org&gt;/&lt;model-a&gt;\", \"&lt;org&gt;/&lt;model-b&gt;\"]\nDATASETS = [\"&lt;org&gt;/&lt;dataset&gt;\"]\n\ndef licences(tags):\n    return [t.split(\":\", 1)[1] for t in (tags or []) if t.startswith(\"license:\")]\n\nfor repo in MODELS:\n    info = api.model_info(repo)\n    print(\"model  \", repo, info.sha[:8], licences(info.tags))\nfor repo in DATASETS:\n    info = api.dataset_info(repo)\n    print(\"dataset\", repo, info.sha[:8], licences(info.tags))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A licence tag of \u003ccode>other\u003c/code> means \"read the LICENSE file\". Record the result in a table that lives in the repository:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Component\u003c/th>\u003cth>Version or revision\u003c/th>\u003cth>Licence\u003c/th>\u003cth>Commercial use?\u003c/th>\u003cth>Obligations\u003c/th>\u003cth>Checked by and date\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Base model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>commit hash\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Apache 2.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep notice\u003c/td>\u003ctd>name, date\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Embedding model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>commit hash\u003c/td>\u003ctd>MIT\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep notice\u003c/td>\u003ctd>name, date\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Chat API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>model version\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider terms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No competing-model training on outputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>name, date\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fine-tuning dataset\u003c/td>\u003ctd>commit hash\u003c/td>\u003ctd>CC BY-NC 4.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>No\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Blocker: replace or relicense\u003c/td>\u003ctd>name, date\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add licence checks to the same review that approves a new model or dataset; involve legal for anything custom.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep notices and attributions in your product's legal page and repository.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Prefer components with clear provenance; \"we found it on a forum\" is not a licence.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you check the licences of every model and dataset in a project?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List every component: base and fine-tuned models, embedding and reranking models, APIs, and every training, fine-tuning, eval and retrieval dataset. For each, pin the exact version or revision, read the licence on that version (Hugging Face metadata tags as a first pass, the LICENSE file or provider terms for custom licences), and record whether commercial use is allowed, any thresholds, use restrictions, attribution, share-alike or output-use conditions. Flag blockers such as non-commercial data or restrictions on training with outputs, record who checked and when, and re-check whenever a version changes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Can you copyright text your app generates without human input in the US?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>According to the US Copyright Office's 2025 report, purely AI-generated material is not copyrightable; only sufficient human contributions such as selection, arrangement or substantial modification can be protected.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a CC BY-SA dataset be a problem in a commercial product?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Share-alike requires derivatives to be released under the same licence. If your product or model counts as a derivative, you may have to license it openly, so get legal advice before using such data.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:human-oversight-and-transparency":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Human oversight\u003c/b> means a competent person can understand, check, override and stop an AI system before its output causes harm. \u003cb>Transparency\u003c/b> means people know when they are dealing with AI, what it can and cannot do, and when content is generated. Both fail quietly when designed badly: approvals that nobody reads, disclosures nobody sees. Good design makes the human's job easy and their decision real.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why oversight fails: automation bias\u003c/h4>\u003cp>People tend to over-trust confident automated output, especially under time pressure. The EU AI Act names this \u003cb>automation bias\u003c/b> and requires high-risk systems to be designed so overseers can:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>understand the system's capabilities and limitations and monitor it;\u003c/li>\u003cli>remain aware of the tendency to over-rely on its output;\u003c/li>\u003cli>correctly interpret its output;\u003c/li>\u003cli>decide not to use it, or disregard, override or reverse its output;\u003c/li>\u003cli>intervene or interrupt it through a \"stop\" button or similar procedure that halts it safely.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Courts have also made clear that a company owns what its AI says. In February 2024 a Canadian tribunal held Air Canada liable for wrong refund advice given by its website chatbot, rejecting the argument that the chatbot was responsible for its own words.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Three levels of oversight\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\u003cth>What the human does\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use when\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Human in the loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Approves each consequential action before it happens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Irreversible or high-value actions: payments, sending external messages, deleting data, decisions about people\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Human on the loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monitors, samples and can intervene or stop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High volume, reversible actions with good monitoring\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Human in command\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Decides whether and where the system is used at all\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Always: someone owns the decision to deploy and can switch it off\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Designing approvals that work\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Show exactly what will happen:\u003c/b> the recipient, the amount, the diff, the record changed, not \"Agent wants to use send_email\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Show why and from what:\u003c/b> the sources or tool results that led to the proposal, with untrusted content clearly marked.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Make edit and reject as easy as approve,\u003c/b> and make the safe option the default.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tier by risk:\u003c/b> auto-approve small, reversible actions; require approval above thresholds; require two people for the most sensitive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Avoid approval fatigue:\u003c/b> if people approve hundreds of requests a day, they stop reading. Reduce the number of approvals by narrowing tools, not by hiding detail.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Record decisions:\u003c/b> who approved what, when and on what information, for audit and for improving the agent.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stopping and undoing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Give operators a \u003cb>kill switch\u003c/b> per feature or agent (a feature flag that disables it in seconds), rate limits and budgets, and where possible an \u003cb>undo window\u003c/b>, for example drafts that send after a delay or soft deletes. Practise using them, as you would a database restore.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Transparency to users\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>What\u003c/th>\u003cth>How\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"You are talking to AI\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Clear label at the start and in the interface; required for chatbots under Article 50 unless obvious\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Generated content\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Machine-readable marking (provenance metadata such as C2PA content credentials, watermarks) and visible labels for deepfakes; the Commission publishes a code of practice and icons for this\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Limits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Plain statements of what it cannot do; \"check important information\" near consequential answers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sources\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Citations users can open, so they can verify\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Escalation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>When a human has taken over, and how to reach one\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Decisions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>For decisions about people, the main reasons and how to contest them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's agent framework (2025) states the same balance as principles: keep humans in control while enabling autonomy, make agent behaviour transparent, align with human values and expectations, protect privacy and secure interactions. In product terms: ask before acting on what matters, show your work, make it easy to say no, and never let the user wonder whether they are talking to a person.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you design user disclosures and override points for an agent that handles customer refunds?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Disclose at the start that the customer is talking to an AI assistant, explain what it can do (check eligibility, propose refunds up to a limit) and offer a route to a human at any time; cite the policy section behind each decision. Let it issue small refunds on clearly eligible orders automatically, but require a staff member to approve anything above the limit, outside policy or after the agent has read untrusted content, with an approval screen showing the order, amount, policy clause and evidence, plus edit and reject buttons. Log every decision and approver, allow refunds to be reversed within a window, and give operators a kill switch and rate limits to stop the agent instantly.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is automation bias and how does design counter it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The tendency to over-trust automated output. Design counters it by showing evidence and uncertainty, making reject and edit as easy as approve, keeping approvals few enough to read properly, and training overseers on the system's limits.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does the Air Canada chatbot case teach product teams?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>That the organisation is responsible for what its AI tells customers. Answers that state policy need grounding, citations and limits, and promises with financial effect need controls.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:governance/bias-and-fairness-testing-for-llm-apps":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>LLM apps can treat people unfairly in three ways: \u003cb>decisions\u003c/b> (scores and recommendations that differ by group), \u003cb>quality of service\u003c/b> (worse answers for some languages, dialects or names) and \u003cb>representation\u003c/b> (stereotypes in generated text or images). You test for them the way you test anything else: build cases that isolate the difference, run them at scale, and compare results across groups with a threshold you decided in advance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where bias enters\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Models learn from human text, which contains historical patterns and stereotypes. Your app adds more: retrieval corpora that cover some products or languages better, prompts written with one kind of user in mind, and eval sets drawn from your most common users. Bias testing is therefore about your \u003ci>system\u003c/i>, not just the model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Method 1: counterfactual testing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Change only the attribute you care about (name, age, pronoun, nationality, dialect) and keep everything else identical. Any consistent change in the output is caused by that attribute. In 2023 Anthropic used this to build \u003cb>discrim-eval\u003c/b>: 70 decision scenarios (loans, housing, hiring and others), each varied by age, gender and race. Without intervention, Claude 2.0 showed both positive and negative discrimination in some settings; prompt interventions, such as stating that discrimination is illegal and asking the model to ignore demographic information, significantly reduced it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A sketch for an LLM-assisted CV screener (\u003ccode>rate\u003c/code> calls your system and parses a 1 to 10 score):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import statistics\n\nTEMPLATE = (\"Rate this applicant for a senior backend engineer role from 1 to 10. \"\n            \"Reply with the number only.\\n\\nName: {name}\\nAge: {age}\\n\\n{cv}\")\n\ndef age_gap(cvs, name=\"Sam Taylor\", ages=(29, 58), repeats=3):\n    diffs = []\n    for cv in cvs:                                  # 50+ realistic CVs\n        young = statistics.mean(rate(TEMPLATE.format(name=name, age=ages[0], cv=cv))\n                                for _ in range(repeats))\n        older = statistics.mean(rate(TEMPLATE.format(name=name, age=ages[1], cv=cv))\n                                for _ in range(repeats))\n        diffs.append(young - older)\n    favour_young = sum(d &gt; 0 for d in diffs) / len(diffs)\n    return statistics.mean(diffs), favour_young\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Repeat for names associated with different groups (published audit studies supply name lists), gender, and any attribute relevant to your users. Because the comparison is paired (same CV, one change), even 50 CVs give a sensitive test; use the significance methods from the Evals module before claiming a gap.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Method 2: disaggregated evaluation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compute your normal quality metrics separately per group: answer correctness per language, groundedness for questions written in different dialects, resolution rate per customer region. Averages hide the group the system fails. If you cannot know a user's group, build eval sets that represent the groups deliberately.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Method 3: outcome rates for decisions\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When a system selects or rejects people, compare \u003cb>selection rates\u003c/b>. US employment practice uses the \u003cb>four-fifths rule\u003c/b> as a red flag: if one group's selection rate is below 80% of the highest group's, investigate. Worked example:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Group A: 40 of 100 applicants advanced -&gt; rate 0.40\nGroup B: 28 of 100 applicants advanced -&gt; rate 0.28\nImpact ratio = 0.28 / 0.40 = 0.70  (below 0.80: potential adverse impact)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>New York City's Local Law 144 requires a yearly independent \u003cb>bias audit\u003c/b> reporting such impact ratios before employers may use automated employment decision tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Method 4: benchmarks and representation checks\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Benchmarks such as \u003cb>BBQ\u003c/b> (Bias Benchmark for QA, 2021) test whether a model falls back on stereotypes when a question is ambiguous and whether stereotypes override evidence when it is not. For generated content, sample outputs for neutral prompts (\"describe a nurse\", \"an image of a CEO\") and review the distribution, with an LLM judge calibrated against human labels for scale.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mitigation\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Remove protected attributes and obvious proxies (names, photos, dates of birth) from inputs when the task does not need them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Score against explicit rubrics rather than open-ended judgement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use prompt interventions and re-test; they help but do not guarantee fairness.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep humans responsible for decisions about people, and monitor outcome rates in production.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Under the EU AI Act, systems used for hiring, credit or education are high-risk with data-governance duties, and the 2026 amendments explicitly allow processing sensitive data to detect and correct bias, with safeguards.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you test an LLM-based CV screener for age bias?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Take 50 or more realistic CVs and create pairs that differ only in the stated age (for example 29 and 58), keeping names and content identical. Run each version several times through the real screening pipeline, record the scores, and compute the mean paired difference and how often the younger version scores higher. Test whether the gap is statistically significant and compare it with a threshold set in advance; repeat for other attributes and for name-based group signals, and record results in the system card.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the four-fifths rule?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A screening heuristic from US employment practice: if a group's selection rate is less than 80% of the rate for the most-selected group, the process may have adverse impact and needs investigation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is disaggregated evaluation needed even without a decision task?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Quality of service can differ by language, dialect or region; an assistant that is excellent on average may give much worse answers to some users, which only per-group metrics reveal.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:governance/classify-five-use-cases-under-the-eu-ai-act":"\u003cp>You will classify five use cases with a fixed procedure, write down obligations and dates, and then compare with the answer key. The value is in practising the reasoning, especially the edge cases. This is a learning exercise, not legal advice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A notes file or spreadsheet with columns: case, role, tier, legal basis (article or annex point), key obligations, who holds them, date applicable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The Annex III page open in another tab.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Copy the procedure:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>For each use case:\n1. Role: provider, deployer or both? Who builds it, under whose name, who uses it?\n2. Is it an AI system? (It infers outputs from inputs; LLM features are.)\n3. Prohibited? Check the Article 5 list. If yes, stop: it cannot be used.\n4. High-risk? A safety component of an Annex I product, or an Annex III use?\n   For Annex III, does an Article 6(3) exception apply (narrow procedural task,\n   improving completed human work, detecting patterns without replacing human\n   review, preparatory task)? Profiling people is always high-risk.\n5. Transparency duties? Interacts with people; generates synthetic content;\n   emotion recognition or biometric categorisation; deepfakes or public-interest text.\n6. Otherwise minimal risk (AI literacy measures still apply).\n7. GPAI duties? Only if you release or substantially modify a general-purpose model.\n8. Write the obligations, who holds them and the date they apply (as of late 2026).\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Classify these five cases:\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A.\u003c/b> A university uses an LLM to grade first-year essays; the grade counts towards the final mark.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>B.\u003c/b> An HR assistant drafts job adverts from a hiring manager's notes; a recruiter edits and publishes them on the company website.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>C.\u003c/b> A voice agent with a realistic synthetic voice phones customers to remind them about overdue invoices and agree payment dates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>D.\u003c/b> A retail chain uses in-store cameras and a model that infers shoppers' ethnicity to choose which ads to show on screens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>E.\u003c/b> An insurer uses an LLM pipeline that reads health questionnaires and sets life insurance premiums.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/li>\u003cli>For each case fill in every column of your table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For each case, write one design change that would move it to a lower tier or reduce its obligations, and one thing that would push it higher.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare with the answer key below, then run one case through the Compliance Checker.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Case\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tier\u003c/th>\u003cth>Basis\u003c/th>\u003cth>Main obligations and dates\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A. Essay grading\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High-risk\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Annex III, education: evaluating learning outcomes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider: full high-risk requirements; university as deployer: oversight by competent staff, logs, informing students, and a fundamental rights impact assessment if it is a public body. From 2 Dec 2027\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>B. Job advert drafting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minimal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not an Annex III use: it neither targets adverts at people nor filters or evaluates candidates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>AI literacy; good practice: check wording for biased language. Targeting adverts or screening applicants would make it high-risk\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>C. Debt-reminder voice agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Transparency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Article 50: interacts with people; generates synthetic audio\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tell callers they are speaking to an AI (since 2 Aug 2026); provider marks audio as AI-generated in a machine-readable way (systems already on the market before 2 Aug 2026 had until 2 Dec 2026). GDPR applies to the personal data\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D. Ethnicity-based ad targeting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prohibited\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Article 5: biometric categorisation inferring race or ethnic origin\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Must not be used; prohibited since 2 Feb 2025\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>E. Life insurance pricing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High-risk\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Annex III, essential services: risk assessment and pricing for life and health insurance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Provider: full high-risk requirements; insurer as deployer: oversight, logs, a fundamental rights impact assessment, explanations for affected people. From 2 Dec 2027. Health data is a GDPR special category: lawful basis, DPIA, limits on solely automated decisions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Typical design changes: in A, AI feedback that a teacher reviews before setting the grade may fit the preparatory-task exception, if documented and genuinely reviewed; in B, adding candidate filtering would push it into high-risk; in C, a clear disclosure at the start of each call is the key control; D cannot be fixed by design, only by not inferring sensitive traits; in E, removing automated pricing does not help if the model still drives the decision.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which case was hardest to classify, and what made it hard?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Usually A or B: whether a system \"evaluates\" people or only prepares work for a human depends on how the output is used in practice, so the classification follows the real workflow and must be documented, not just the feature description.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does case C have obligations even though it is not high-risk?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Article 50 transparency duties apply to systems that interact with people and generate synthetic content, independent of the risk tier: callers must be told they are talking to AI and the synthetic audio must be marked.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:ai-risk-assessment":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Pick one real AI use case: a feature your team runs or plans, or Atlas as an optional extension (this becomes \"Atlas's system card\"). Produce the package that a compliance reviewer, a security team or an enterprise customer would ask for before approving launch. Write it for a reader who has never seen the system.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use-case description:\u003c/b> purpose, users, affected people, out-of-scope uses, decision the system supports.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Legal classification:\u003c/b> EU AI Act role and tier with the articles or annex points and reasoning, dates that apply as of late 2026, and other relevant law (GDPR, sector rules, local laws such as employment bias-audit rules).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Risk register\u003c/b> structured by the NIST AI RMF: at least 12 risks drawn from NIST AI 600-1, the OWASP lists and your own error analysis, each with owner, likelihood, impact, controls, metric and status.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data-flow diagram\u003c/b> showing personal data, processors and regions, retention and deletion paths, plus a DPIA screening (is a full DPIA needed, and why?).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Licence inventory\u003c/b> of every model, API and dataset with versions and blocking terms flagged.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fairness test:\u003c/b> at least one counterfactual or disaggregated evaluation, with results and a decision.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>System card\u003c/b> using the template from the model and system cards lesson.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Oversight and transparency design:\u003c/b> disclosures, approval points, override and stop mechanisms.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mitigation and monitoring plan:\u003c/b> launch gates, metrics and thresholds, alerts, review cadence, incident response, owner.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Write the use-case description and draw the system and data-flow diagrams.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Classify under the EU AI Act; cross-check with the Compliance Checker and note any disagreement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the risk register: Govern entries first (owner, inventory, policies), then Map, Measure and Manage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compile the licence inventory and resolve or escalate blockers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the fairness test and record the results and decision.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the oversight and transparency design.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the system card and the monitoring plan; finish with a one-page summary for a decision-maker.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A reviewer unfamiliar with the system can answer from your package: what it does and for whom, its legal classification and why, the top five risks and their controls, what is measured and how often, and who is accountable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every high-impact risk has a control and a metric, not just a description.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The system card, register and inventory live in the repository with dates and versions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Map your controls to the ISO/IEC 42001 Annex A objectives and draft a Statement of Applicability.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Automate a quarterly review reminder and a CI check that the system card's model versions match the deployed configuration.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run a second fairness test on a different attribute or language and compare.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Be concrete: \"groundedness at least 90% on 200 questions, checked nightly\" is a control; \"we will monitor quality\" is not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Record uncertainty honestly. \"Classification depends on whether staff review every grade; confirm with legal\" is better than a confident guess.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reuse what earlier modules produced: evals (Evals module), traces and dashboards (Observability), red-team results (Security).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
